INDEX -- what is on this disk, and where it came from======================================================Every command here was tested with THIS DISK AS THE ONLY DISK (os9exec/dd/CMDS/sh), no Microware module present anywhere -- no shell, csl, cio ormath.  Note this disk does not "boot": os9exec is the kernel and simply runsa program with this disk mounted as /dd.  *  A STAR before a name means that program needs one of Microware's     runtime trap modules -- cio, and for some math or csl.  IT STILL RUNS     HERE: those five modules are on this disk, in CMDS, included with     Microware's permission (SOURCES.txt records the grant).  The star now     tells you what a program depends on, not what it lacks.     The grid at the foot of this file is the authority, and its own     header carries the count -- READ IT THERE.  It was measured by     RUNNING every program against a disk with the five modules     removed, never inferred from a `cio' string in a binary, which     is wrong in both directions.  Its names match rather more     program FILES than there are names, because a few names appear     in more than one directory.     As re-measured 2026-08-31: 365 names, 368 files; of the 942     programs under CMDS, 368 are starred and 574 need no module at     all; of the 597 in CMDS and CMDS/GAMES, 290 are starred and 307     need nothing.  Every one of those figures was wrong in this     paragraph until that day, including the count of the grid two     lines below it.  The counts move as the collection grows --     tools/measure_layout.py and DOC/CATEGORIES are generated and are     always current; a number typed into prose is not, and this     paragraph is the proof.  THREE PROGRAMS ARE BASIC09, not 68000 code: bio, blackjack and  wysetime.  They are I-code modules (type 2, language 2) and need  Microware's runb to interpret them, the way a starred program needs  cio.  Run them by BARE NAME with runb loaded -- `load runb` then  `bio`.  Giving runb a pathname instead raises BASIC09 error 43,  "Unknown procedure", which reads like a broken program and is not.  BASIC09's own codes are 10-80 and are not OS-9 error numbers.  SYS/errmsg carries text for them anyway -- 67 entries, 000:010 to  000:080 -- so `error 43' reads as "Unknown procedure" rather than as  nothing at all.These are not broken and they are not going anywhere.  They were compiled theordinary way for the era (cc -qixm), which is a licensing question, not aquality one.Provenance: everything came from the Microware OS-9 hobbyist archive, theHamburg OS-9 Users Group, and other public preservation sites. The originalarchives are kept in CMDS/archives/ -- ed, elvis and grep include full Csource in there. Per-package documentation is in DOC/ (see DOC/README).SOURCES.txt carries fuller per-command provenance and licences.One command here is definitely OUR OWN BUILD, not an archive binary: ls, ourrepaired build of GNU fileutils 3.13, which now builds from SRC/ls on thisdisk.  wc was long listed beside it and its provenance is unsettled -- seeits entry below.  No source for it has been found anywhere.This paragraph used to name five, and to say cat, basename and dirname hadbeen rebuilt against os9lib so they would not need cio. That was wrong twiceover, and the star grid below always said so -- all three are starred, so allthree DO need cio. They are archive binaries, byte-for-byte: cat, basenameand dirname came from Martin Gregorie's sh75 package, along with sh itself.Corrected 2026-08-24 by comparing them with that archive.------------------------------------------------------ *break          send a BREAK on a serial line (assembler example) -- and                 under os9exec it reaches F$SysDbg and stops the EMULATOR                 in its own debugger, waiting for an answer.  In a script                 that is a hang  btop          convert characters to bit patterns -- its own Function: line,         